<Base title="Internet Africa to UUNET SA | Colin Pinkham">
<main class="case">
  <section class="shell case-hero">
    <a class="case-back" href="/work">← All work</a>
    <div class="eyebrow">Selected Work · 1996–2003</div>
    <h1>Internet Africa to UUNET SA</h1>
    <p class="case-lead">The period when the enthusiasm of youth became a major business, with rapid growth in South Africa, expansion across Africa, and a leadership team learning how to scale an organisation at speed.</p>
    <div class="case-meta"><span>Organisation</span><strong>Internet Africa, UUNET SA</strong><span>Focus</span><strong>Product, commercial strategy, Africa</strong></div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-grid">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">The experience</div>
      <h2>Growing up professionally while the business was growing up around us.</h2>
      <p>UUNET Africa was a formative period. The leadership team was still young, the average age of the wider organisation was young, and the business was expanding at a remarkable pace.</p>
      <p>There was a particular energy to that combination. People were learning quickly, taking on responsibilities that were growing faster than the organisation could formalise them, and helping build a business that was moving from the early Internet market into a substantial corporate operation.</p>
      <p>For me, this was the point where hands on network engineering developed into product development, business development, commercial thinking and leadership across several African markets.</p>
    </article>
    <aside class="case-panel">
      <div class="eyebrow">A rapidly changing organisation</div>
      <div class="step"><b>01</b> Internet Africa was becoming UUNET SA</div>
      <div class="step"><b>02</b> The corporate ISP market was expanding</div>
      <div class="step"><b>03</b> Product and management functions became formalised</div>
      <div class="step"><b>04</b> Operations expanded into Africa</div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-grid reverse">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">Documented work</div>
      <h2>Moving beyond the network into product and commercial leadership.</h2>
      <p>The formal career record describes progression into Senior Executive Manager for Product and Business Development and then Director for Africa, with responsibility extending across Kenya, Namibia, Botswana, Zambia and SDN Mauritius.</p>
      <p>A major part of the work was creating formal product development and product management functions, while network functions were handed over to specialist teams.</p>
      <div class="tags"><span>Product development</span><span>Business development</span><span>Commercial modelling</span><span>Africa</span></div>
    </article>
    <aside class="case-panel image-panel"><img src="/images/uia-logo.jpg" alt="Internet Africa historical logo"><div class="caption">Internet Africa historical material</div></aside>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-grid">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">Commercial discipline</div>
      <h2>Turning network economics into products and decisions.</h2>
      <p>The work included detailed costing and pricing models, as well as models for network utilisation and amortisation. These supported forecasting, budgeting, procurement of capacity and hardware, and product pricing.</p>
      <p>The original account records that these models continued to be used after this period, including through to departure in 2014.</p>
    </article>
    <aside class="case-note"><strong>The important transition</strong><p>The technology remained important, but the role increasingly required understanding how technology became a product, how a product became a business, and how that business could scale.</p></aside>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-grid reverse">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">Expansion across Africa</div>
      <h2>Growth was not only about scale in South Africa.</h2>
      <p>The period included expansion into multiple African markets and participation in technology due diligence associated with acquisitions, including Africa Online.</p>
      <p>That combination of operating experience, commercial analysis and African market exposure provided a bridge into the global telecommunications environment that followed.</p>
    </article>
